<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Istakhr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stakhr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Istakhr was an ancient city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, a few kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>. After the Achaemenid period it became the main city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>, and in the early 3rd century CE it was the home of the Sasanian family, whose temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a> stood there. It remained the chief city of Fars until the Arab conquest of the 7th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> replaced it, and was later abandoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istakhr.jpg" alt="Istakhr" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: درفش کاویانی (Kaviani flag) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estakhr_Darafsh_(55).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
